NpuEmbeddings -- BERT-family embeddings on the AMD NPU (XDNA1/npu1,
XDNA2/npu2; --dev selects the generation)

  This is npuimage.

    npuembeddings   embed, list, add, tokenize, serve
    npuaudio        transcribe, serve
    npuimage        classify, pose, hands, mppose, ocr, serve

  npuimage serve <model> [--port N] [--bind ADDR]
        the HTTP endpoint for WHATEVER the container is. One verb,
        four endpoints, and the model's arch picks which:
          /v1/embeddings           a BERT-family text model
          /v1/audio/transcriptions Whisper
          /v1/classify             a ViT classifier
          /v1/pose                 YOLOv8-pose
        Downloads and verifies the model first if it is not
        installed yet. The mode is chosen by the container's arch, never
        by a flag, and each endpoint answers only for its own path -- a
        wrong path is a 404 naming the right one, never another
        endpoint's answer.
        Only this binary's own family is served here (npuimage): another
        binary's container is refused by name before the listener opens,
        rather than answered by an endpoint this process is not named for.

  npuimage classify <model> <image.png> [more.png ...]
        classify images with a ViT model (vit-base-patch16-224), one
        per argument. The label goes to stdout on its own and the
        status block to stderr, so it pipes. PNG and JPEG only --
        anything else is refused by name rather than guessed at, and
        so is a non-square resize or a shortest-edge crop: the
        container says which geometry it was trained with and this
        reads it. --top-k prints the runners-up to stderr, --json
        the objects to stdout. `embed` is not how this runs.
        Over HTTP: `npuimage serve <model>`, which answers POST
        /v1/classify with the same object `classify --json` prints (both
        call npue::vit::prediction_json), one `image` part and an
        optional `top_k`. GET /v1/labels returns the whole vocabulary, so
        a client can turn an id into a name without the container.

  npuimage pose <model> <image.png> [more.png ...]
        detect people and their 17 COCO keypoints with a YOLOv8-pose
        model. Same shape as classify and for the same reasons: one
        image per argument, the result on stdout and the status block
        on stderr. --json prints one object per image with every box,
        every joint, the 12-edge skeleton and the letterbox transform,
        --text a human summary. --conf/--iou/--kpt/--max-det move the
        thresholds the container was not given. --pose-dump FILE
        writes every graph node's fp32 output, for diffing against an
        independent interpreter.
        CPU BY DEFAULT, and that is a measurement rather than a
        preference. --npu-ops conv moves the 72 convolutions to
        the array and nothing else, needs a container packed with --npu
        and a design set, and is SLOWER here: 258 ms of network on 16
        threads against 384 ms dispatched as GEMMs, because N must be a
        multiple of 128 (2.6x arithmetic padding waste before anything
        runs), the stem alone is 100 of the 436 dispatches, and a
        dispatch costs 660 us of which 140 us is the device's GEMM. Per
        layer 19 of the 72 are faster on the array and an oracle
        splitting each onto its faster side measured 146 ms -- 2.6%.
        The status block prints both sides, per image.
        MEASURED on the CPU path, 640x640 input, i8: 29 ms front end,
        258 ms network of which 88 ms is the GEMM, 57 ms im2col, 10 ms
        the weight transpose and 7 ms the output transpose, 1 ms decode.

        The same model over HTTP: `npuimage serve <model>`, which
        answers POST /v1/pose. Multipart with one `image` part (PNG or
        JPEG, by magic bytes) and optional conf, iou, kpt, max_det. The
        answer is byte for byte what `pose --json` prints -- both call
        npue::pose::result_json -- so the endpoint and the CLI cannot
        drift. Also GET /health and GET /v1/models. Per-request
        thresholds apply to that request only.

  <model> in any of this binary's verbs is either a NAME -- looked up
        in models/, fetched and verified against the catalogue's sha256
        when the catalogue knows it -- or a PATH to a .npum, used as
        given and never re-resolved by name:
            npuimage classify models/vit-base-patch16-224.npum image.png
        The runtime names a path-held model after its file, which is
        also how it finds runtime/<stem>/artifacts_npu<N>.

  Options for this binary's verbs:
    --port N          listen port (default 8080)
    --bind ADDR       interface (default 127.0.0.1, localhost only)
    --threads N       host thread budget (default 24 for these)
    --pipeline N      concurrent encode lanes (serve/embed pass 4;
                      1 lane = no pipelining. A later --pipeline on the
                      command line wins, so --pipeline 1 disables it)
    --artifacts DIR   override the design set
    --npu-ops CODES
                      send the listed ops to the ARRAY instead of the
                      host, comma-separated. An op not listed runs on
                      the host, so there is no inverse flag. The
                      default -- nothing listed -- runs every
                      op in this process; for most codes that
                      is the measured-faster side, and for
                      gemm it is not.
                        gemm   the four per-layer GEMMs
                        gelu   GELU (exact erf; a Whisper
                               container declares this one)
                        layn   LayerNorm
                        softm  softmax
                        conv   Whisper's conv1/conv2
                        attn   attention, as QK^T and softmax.V GEMMs
                        mproj  the slaney mel bank, as a GEMM
                        fft    the 400-point transform, as a GEMM
                        logit  the tied-embedding projection, in
                               eight chunks of the vocabulary
                      The same nine codes in every architecture;
                      which of them yours can honour is in
                      NPU_OPS.md, and a code that cannot is refused
                      by name with the reason, never ignored.
                      Worth asking for? Measured, per 3 s of
                      whisper-tiny on 16 threads: conv 0.94 -> <S> s
                      wall and 5.5 -> <S> s CPU; gemm 1.06 -> <S> s
                      on an embedder and 0.435 -> <S> s on a ViT.
                      Every other code is either neutral or slower.
                      README, 'What to move to the NPU'.
                      The exporter's flag is the SAME STRING and it
                      BUILDS what this one selects. gelu/layn/softm
                      write a sibling design set each (gelu/,
                      layernorm/, softmax/) and cost one extra
                      hw_context EACH; gemm/conv/attn/mproj/fft/
                      logit are streams inside the GEMM sets and
                      cost none.
                      A missing design is refused by name, never
                      silently run on the host -- and a run that
                      would exceed the context budget is refused
                      before any context is built unless
                      --allow-contention says the contention is
                      intended (see below).
                      The speech-to-text codes are refused on an
                      embedder. Replaces --npu-ops, --npu-eltwise and
                      --host-ln/--host-sm/--host-gelu, which are now
                      refused by name so a stale command line cannot
                      look like it worked.
    --allow-contention
                      proceed even though another process holds NPU
                      hw_contexts. Without it, a run that would exceed
                      the device's context budget is refused before any
                      context is built, and a timed run refuses to
                      report a throughput. ANY NUMBER FROM A CONTENDED
                      RUN IS NOT AN NPU PERFORMANCE CLAIM.
    --dev npu1|npu2   the NPU generation this process runs on. A
                      design set built for the other generation is
                      refused, not loaded. Defaults to NPU_DEVICE, or
                      NPU2=1, or npu1
    --language CODE   transcribe: the language to prime the decoder
                      with (en, de, ru, ...). There is no detection
                      and no default worth having: an assumed language
                      is a fluent transcript of the wrong language, so
                      it defaults to en and says so on stderr. A code
                      the model's tokenizer has no token for is
                      refused, never approximated.
    --task NAME      transcribe: 'transcribe' (default) or
                      'translate' -- English output either way
    --convert        transcribe: ingest through ffmpeg instead of the
                      WAV reader. The reader refuses what it cannot
                      represent (a second channel, 8-bit, 44.1 kHz)
                      by name; this is the way in for those
    --max-new N      transcribe: cap on generated tokens per window
                      (default: the model's own position bound, 448)
    --chunk-seconds N  transcribe: window length, default 30 -- the
                      model's own. Longer is refused: the encoder has
                      weights for a fixed number of positions and a
                      cut window is a transcript of the first 30 s
                      presented as the whole recording
    --stride-seconds N transcribe: overlap on EACH side of a window,
                      default 5 (transformers' own), so windows start
                      chunk - 2*stride apart and the merge
                      de-duplicates the overlap
    --json           transcribe: print {"text", "segments", ...}
    --top-k          classify: print the runners-up to stderr, under
                      the image. The whole row is ranked and the top
                      ten shown -- a truncated list reads as "nothing
                      else is close", which is a different claim
    --root DIR        override where models/ and the design live
    --token VALUE     HuggingFace access token for a GATED model
                      (falls back to the HF_TOKEN env var if omitted)
    --prefix NAME     task prefix to prepend to every input text, for
                      `embed` and `--bench` only. REQUIRED for a model
                      whose container carries a prompt table (nomic
                      wants 'search_document' or 'search_query';
                      EmbeddingGemma has 14) -- there is no default,
                      because a wrongly-prefixed embedding is
                      correctly shaped and correctly normed, so
                      nothing downstream can tell it is wrong. Pass
                      --prefix "" for no prefix at all. An unknown
                      NAME refuses and lists the real options, and so
                      does passing one to a model with no table (the
                      four BERT models). Always printed on stderr.
                      `serve` REJECTS this flag: its prompt is chosen
                      per request -- POST "prompt_name", and GET
                      /health lists what the model accepts.
    --allow-truncation
                      embed the first `seq` tokens of an input that
                      is too long, instead of refusing it. OFF by
                      default: a truncated text still returns a
                      correctly shaped, correctly normed vector, so
                      nothing downstream can tell that the answer is
                      wrong -- and inputs sharing a preamble truncate
                      to IDENTICAL vectors. Without this flag such an
                      input is an error naming its real token count;
                      with it, a one-line warning on stderr.
                      This build runs at the sequence length its
                      design was exported for -- see
                      tools/export/export_gemm_rtp.py --seq to build for a
                      longer one.

  The flag form is unchanged and still works:
    npuimage <root> --model NAME --artifacts DIR --serve [port]
  and carries the probes and benchmarks; see docs/CURRENT_STATUS.md.

